WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.830 --> 00:00:04.270
Earlier, we saw resource records inside a
DNS server.

00:00:04.270 --> 00:00:08.280
You may, however, at times need to look up
some DNS information on an entity, and you

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:10.620
may not have a DNS server in front of you.

00:00:10.620 --> 00:00:13.480
For this, we can use the nslookup command.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:18.570
I have opened a command prompt, and I am going
to type, nslookup, and select the Enter key

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:19.570
on the keyboard.

00:00:19.570 --> 00:00:22.529
The result is a DNS server address to which
I am connected.

00:00:22.529 --> 00:00:28.570
Doing this also puts us in nslookup mode,
where we can look up DNS information on other

00:00:28.570 --> 00:00:29.640
entities.

00:00:29.640 --> 00:00:36.500
For instance, if I type, google.com, and select
the Enter key on the keyboard, I have DNS

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:38.720
information from Google.

00:00:38.720 --> 00:00:42.220
By default, nslookup returns A type records.

00:00:42.220 --> 00:00:45.980
However, let's say I want to see the mail
exchange record for an entity.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:54.520
I will type, set type=mx, to have nslookup
look up mail exchange records.

00:00:54.520 --> 00:01:01.040
Then, I will type, google.com, again, and
this time, I see mail exchange records.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:07.360
When I'm done using nslookup, I can just type,
exit, select the Enter key on the keyboard,

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:09.100
and exit nslookup.

